# 23. Relationship Validity

A relationship is valid when:

1. the source exists;
2. the target exists;
3. the relationship type is defined;
4. the source is permitted by the relationship;
5. the target is permitted by the relationship;
6. cardinality constraints are satisfied;
7. lifecycle constraints are satisfied;
8. no applicable Classification constraint is violated.

---

# 28. Relationship Vocabulary

The Classification component shall maintain a controlled vocabulary of canonical relationship types.

The vocabulary may include:

- `contains`;
- `belongsTo`;
- `supersedes`;
- `replaces`;
- other formally approved relationships.

Only relationship types approved through Classification Governance become canonical.

---

# 40. Relationship Validation

Classification Validation evaluates whether relationships conform to the approved model.

Validation may verify:

- source validity;
- target validity;
- relationship type;
- direction;
- cardinality;
- constraints;
- lifecycle state;
- integrity.

Validation does not authorize new relationship semantics.

---

# 55. Relationship Dictionary

The Classification Dictionary shall provide authoritative definitions for canonical relationship types.

A relationship definition should include:

- relationship name;
- semantic definition;
- source;
- target;
- inverse;
- cardinality;
- constraints;
- lifecycle semantics.

---

# 57. Extensibility

The Classification Relationship model may be extended when new semantic relationships are required.

Extensions shall:

- have explicit semantics;
- define source and target;
- define direction;
- define cardinality;
- define constraints;
- preserve existing semantics;
- receive governance approval.

This follows the Classification Extensibility model.

---

# 65. Conformance

A Classification implementation conforms to this document when:

- Classification Relationships are explicitly defined;
- canonical relationship types have defined semantics;
- source and target roles are defined;
- relationship direction is defined;
- relationship constraints are enforceable;
- hierarchical relationships preserve Classification structure;
- invalid relationships can be detected;
- relationship evolution is governed;
- historical relationships can remain traceable;
- implementation remains independent of the conceptual model.

---

# 12. Family Constraints

A Family represents a high-level Classification domain.

A Family shall:

- have a unique canonical identity;
- have a defined semantic meaning;
- not duplicate another Family semantically;
- remain independent of implementation;
- participate only in permitted relationships.

---

# 13. Type Constraints

A Type specializes Classification within a Family context.

A Type shall:

- have a unique canonical identity;
- have a defined semantic meaning;
- belong to a permitted Family;
- not duplicate another Type within the same semantic context;
- comply with the Classification Schema.

---

# 14. Class Constraints

A Class provides a more specific Classification level within a Type context.

A Class shall:

- have a unique canonical identity;
- have a defined semantic meaning;
- belong to a permitted Type;
- comply with applicable constraints;
- not introduce semantic duplication.

---

# 26. Classification Element Identity Constraints

Every canonical Classification Element shall possess a stable identity.

The identity shall be:

- unique within the applicable scope;
- stable;
- traceable;
- independent of physical representation.

Changing the physical representation of a Classification Element shall not implicitly create a new conceptual element.

---

# 27. Identity Stability

Classification Element identity shall remain stable throughout its lifecycle unless Governance explicitly authorizes a replacement.

The following are not, by themselves, identity changes:

- renaming a file;
- changing storage;
- changing serialization;
- changing repository;
- changing implementation.

---

# 28. Cardinality Constraints

Classification cardinality defines the permitted number of relationships or elements within a structural context.

Cardinality may constrain:

- number of parents;
- number of children;
- number of relationships;
- number of applicable Classification Elements.

Cardinality shall be defined independently of implementation.

---

# 48. Constraint Identity

Every canonical Classification Constraint shall possess a stable architectural identity.

A constraint identity allows:

- traceability;
- validation reporting;
- governance decisions;
- historical preservation;
- change tracking.

---

# 55. Constraint Scope

Every constraint shall have a defined scope.

Possible scopes include:

- Family;
- Type;
- Class;
- Relationship;
- Schema;
- Lifecycle;
- entire Classification component.

A constraint shall not be interpreted beyond its defined scope.

---

# 57. Constraint Violation

A Classification artifact violates this specification when one or more applicable constraints are not satisfied.

Typical violations include:

- duplicated Classification Elements;
- conflicting definitions;
- semantic inconsistencies;
- invalid hierarchy;
- invalid relationships;
- invalid cardinality;
- circular dependencies;
- invalid lifecycle transitions;
- orphan relationships;
- unauthorized Classification evolution.

This parallels the established Metadata constraint model.

---

# 47. Governance Responsibility

Classification Governance is responsible for controlling changes to constraints.

Governance may:

- introduce constraints;
- modify constraints;
- deprecate constraints;
- retire constraints;
- authorize constraint evolution.

---

# 39. Extension Activation

An approved extension becomes Active when all required conditions have been satisfied.

These may include:

- validation;
- documentation;
- Dictionary update;
- Specification update;
- migration;
- governance conditions.

---

# 40. Extension Deprecation

An extension may be deprecated when:

- its capability is obsolete;
- a superior extension replaces it;
- the underlying architectural requirement disappears;
- the extension introduces unacceptable long-term complexity.

Deprecation shall preserve historical traceability.

---

# 44. Extension and Migration

Some extensions may require migration.

Migration may include:

- reclassification;
- relationship transformation;
- Schema migration;
- Dictionary updates;
- validation updates.

Migration shall preserve historical meaning.

---

# 25. Governance Approval

Every extension intended to become canonical shall be evaluated by Classification Governance.

Governance shall consider:

- architectural consistency;
- semantic preservation;
- structural integrity;
- compatibility;
- impact;
- maintainability;
- long-term architectural consequences.

Only approved extensions become part of the canonical Classification architecture.

This separation between validation and authorization is consistent with the established Metadata model.

---

# 34. Governance Decision

Classification Governance may:

- approve;
- approve with conditions;
- request modification;
- defer;
- reject.

The decision shall be traceable.

---

# 37. Conditional Approval

Governance may approve an extension subject to conditions.

For example:

- additional validation;
- migration;
- documentation;
- compatibility changes;
- lifecycle restrictions.

The extension becomes canonical only when the approval conditions are satisfied.

---

# 49. Extension and Classification Governance

Classification Governance controls the introduction and evolution of extensions.

Governance evaluates:

- architectural consistency;
- semantic preservation;
- compatibility;
- impact;
- maintainability;
- long-term consequences.

